        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2025-06-17T18:52:22Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Both 'mini.clue' and 'mini.keymap' are meant to work with mappings. This is far from \"showing any key press\".\r\n\r\nNeovim itself has a pretty good (since Neovim 0.11) built-in way of acting on every keypress: `:h vim.on_key()`. It can be used without any plugin to show typed keys with something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal do_show_keys = false\r\nlocal ns_id = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('show-keys')\r\n\r\n_G.toggle_show_keys = function()\r\n  do_show_keys = not do_show_keys\r\n  if not do_show_keys then return vim.on_key(nil, ns_id) end\r\n  vim.on_key(function(_, typed)\r\n    if typed ~= '' then vim.notify(vim.fn.keytrans(typed)) end\r\n  end, ns_id)\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nExecuting `:lua _G.toggle_show_keys()` starts to show with `vim.notify()` key presses if not showing, stops otherwise. It is not without its quirks, but works pretty well. Together with 'vim.notify = require('mini.notify').make_notify()' it shows a \"trail\" of keys also (as notifications will be shown for some time until removed).\r\n\r\nBut I'd strongly suggest using a higher level key casting tool, as it will be more robust. I prefer [screenkey](https://www.thregr.org/wavexx/software/screenkey/).",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Both 'mini.clue' and 'mini.keymap' are meant to work with mappings. This is far from \"showing any key press\".</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Neovim itself has a pretty good (since Neovim 0.11) built-in way of acting on every keypress: <code class=\"notranslate\">:h vim.on_key()</code>. It can be used without any plugin to show typed keys with something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local do_show_keys = false\nlocal ns_id = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('show-keys')\n\n_G.toggle_show_keys = function()\n  do_show_keys = not do_show_keys\n  if not do_show_keys then return vim.on_key(nil, ns_id) end\n  vim.on_key(function(_, typed)\n    if typed ~= '' then vim.notify(vim.fn.keytrans(typed)) end\n  end, ns_id)\nend\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">do_show_keys</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">false</span>\n<span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">ns_id</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">api</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">nvim_create_namespace</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>show-keys<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)\n\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">_G</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">toggle_show_keys</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>()\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">do_show_keys</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">not</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">do_show_keys</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">not</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">do_show_keys</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">then</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">on_key</span>(<span class=\"pl-c1\">nil</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">ns_id</span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">on_key</span>(<span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">_</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">typed</span>)\n    <span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">typed</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">~=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span><span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">then</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">notify</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">fn</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">keytrans</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">typed</span>)) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">ns_id</span>)\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Executing <code class=\"notranslate\">:lua _G.toggle_show_keys()</code> starts to show with <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.notify()</code> key presses if not showing, stops otherwise. It is not without its quirks, but works pretty well. Together with 'vim.notify = require('mini.notify').make_notify()' it shows a \"trail\" of keys also (as notifications will be shown for some time until removed).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">But I'd strongly suggest using a higher level key casting tool, as it will be more robust. I prefer <a href=\"https://www.thregr.org/wavexx/software/screenkey/\" rel=\"nofollow\">screenkey</a>.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Both 'mini.clue' and 'mini.keymap' are meant to work with mappings. This is far from \"showing any key press\".\nNeovim itself has a pretty good (since Neovim 0.11) built-in way of acting on every keypress: :h vim.on_key(). It can be used without any plugin to show typed keys with something like this:\nlocal do_show_keys = false\nlocal ns_id = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('show-keys')\n\n_G.toggle_show_keys = function()\n  do_show_keys = not do_show_keys\n  if not do_show_keys then return vim.on_key(nil, ns_id) end\n  vim.on_key(function(_, typed)\n    if typed ~= '' then vim.notify(vim.fn.keytrans(typed)) end\n  end, ns_id)\nend\nExecuting :lua _G.toggle_show_keys() starts to show with vim.notify() key presses if not showing, stops otherwise. It is not without its quirks, but works pretty well. Together with 'vim.notify = require('mini.notify').make_notify()' it shows a \"trail\" of keys also (as notifications will be shown for some time until removed).\nBut I'd strongly suggest using a higher level key casting tool, as it will be more robust. I prefer screenkey.",
